    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[22].scaleEnum
      value: 22
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[23].scaleEnum
      value: 23
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[7].description
      value: "A principal maqam in Arabic music, Bay\u0101t\u012B traditionally features
        quarter-tone intervals that give it a distinctive melancholic warmth. In
        twelve-tone equal temperament, it closely resembles the Phrygian mode, widely
        used across Middle Eastern and North African musical traditions."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[8].description
      value: Built on the minor pentatonic with the addition of the 'blue note' (a
        flatted fifth), the Blues scale is the foundation of blues, rock, and jazz
        improvisation. The chromatic tension of the blue note gives the scale its
        characteristic gritty expressiveness.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[9].description
      value: A minor mode distinguished by its raised sixth degree, giving it a brighter,
        more hopeful quality than the natural minor. Widely used in jazz, folk, and
        modal rock music, from Miles Davis to traditional Celtic melodies.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[10].description
      value: ' Differs from the natural minor by raising the seventh degree, creating
        an augmented second interval that gives the scale its dramatic, exotic character.
        Prevalent in classical composition, flamenco, and various Middle Eastern
        and Eastern European traditions.'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[11].description
      value: "Also known as the Hungarian Minor or Gypsy Minor scale, features two
        augmented seconds that give it a darkly ornamental and passionate character.
        Strongly associated with Roma musical traditions and Hungarian classical
        composers such as Liszt and Bart\xF3k."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[12].description
      value: A Japanese pentatonic scale used in shakuhachi flute music, the Insen
        scale has a contemplative, austere quality. Its combination of a minor second
        and open fourths creates a sense of spacious melancholy characteristic of
        Zen-influenced musical aesthetics.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[13].description
      value: A Japanese pentatonic scale, the Iwato is darker and more dissonant
        than the Insen, featuring both a minor second and a tritone. Traditionally
        associated with Buddhist chant and theatrical music, it evokes an atmosphere
        of mystery and stillness.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[14].description
      value: An octatonic scale alternating whole and half steps, famously employed
        by Rimsky-Korsakov and later by Stravinsky, Messiaen, and other composers.
        Its symmetrical structure creates a shimmering, ambiguous tonality often
        used to evoke the magical or supernatural.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[15].description
      value: A major mode with a raised fourth degree, giving it a bright, dreamy,
        and slightly ethereal quality. Favoured in film scoring and jazz, the Lydian
        mode appears in the work of composers from Beethoven to John Williams and
        is central to George Russell's Lydian Chromatic Concept.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[16].description
      value: ' A five-note scale omitting the second and sixth degrees of the natural
        minor, creating an open, versatile sound free of semitone tension. One of
        the most universal scales in world music, found in traditions from West Africa
        to East Asia to American blues and rock.'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[17].description
      value: The major pentatonic removes the fourth and seventh degrees of the major
        scale, eliminating all semitones to produce a bright, consonant, and universally
        appealing sound. Found independently across countless musical cultures worldwide,
        from Chinese traditional music to Scottish folk song.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[18].description
      value: A dramatic and richly ornamented scale featuring two instances of consecutive
        semitones and an augmented second. Evokes the grandeur and complexity of
        classical Persian music, with a tense, regal character distinct from Arabic
        or Turkish modal systems.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[19].description
      value: A dramatic and richly ornamented scale featuring two instances of consecutive
        semitones and an augmented second. Evokes the grandeur and complexity of
        classical Persian music, with a tense, regal character distinct from Arabic
        or Turkish modal systems.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[20].description
      value: The simplest expression of major tonality, consisting of a root, major
        third, and perfect fifth. The foundational building block of Western harmony,
        providing a bright, stable, and resolved sound.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[21].description
      value: The minor counterpart to the major triad, consisting of a root, minor
        third, and perfect fifth. Conveys a darker, more introspective quality while
        retaining the structural stability of a perfect fifth.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[22].description
      value: Also known as the 'Acoustic' or 'Overtone' scale, differs from the major
        scale in having a raised fourth and lowered seventh. Traditionally, the scale
        persists in the music of peoples of South Siberia, especially in Tuvan music.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[23].description
      value: A symmetrical hexatonic scale built entirely of whole steps, with no
        semitones or leading tones. Its uniform structure creates a floating, unresolved,
        dreamlike quality famously exploited by Debussy and used widely in film and
        game music to suggest wonder or disorientation.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5612154351421515037, guid: b47363fc3813c0d4a85d57e623b0f67e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: scales.Array.data[7].intervals.Array.size
      value: 7
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
